{
  "image_name": "0a04358b6ed158cee41a7bdb2a142d32.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two routes by which damaged mitochondria are targeted for autophagic removal. Under normal conditions, PINK1 is imported through the TOM/TIM23 machinery into mitochondria, processed by proteases such as MPP and PARL, and then degraded by the proteasome, so it does not accumulate on the outer membrane.\n\nWhen mitochondria become damaged, this import/processing pathway appears blocked, and PINK1 builds up on the mitochondrial surface. There it becomes phosphorylated and promotes recruitment and activation of Parkin. Activated Parkin then ubiquitinates multiple outer mitochondrial membrane proteins, creating ubiquitin signals that are further amplified. These ubiquitin tags are recognized by adaptor proteins that bind LC3-II on the growing phagophore, linking the impaired mitochondrion to the autophagic membrane and promoting mitophagy.\n\nThe lower part of the figure shows a second, receptor-mediated mechanism. Outer membrane receptors such as BNIP3, FUNDC1, and NIX, shown in phosphorylated states, directly interact with LC3-II on the phagophore without relying on Parkin-dependent ubiquitination. In both cases, the phagophore expands around the mitochondrion and closes to form an autophagosome, enclosing the organelle for degradation.",
